<!-- #region lms -->

| Step | Working | Result |
|---|---|---|
| predict | V̂ = w₀ + w₁·f₁ = 0 + 0 × 4 | 0 |
| error | V_train − V̂ = 10 − 0 | 10 |
| update w₁ | w₁ + c·f₁·error = 0 + 0.1 × 4 × 10 | **4** |
| update w₀ | w₀ + c·1·error = 0 + 0.1 × 1 × 10 | **1** |
| re-check | V̂ = 1 + 4 × 4 | 17 |

The prediction jumped from 0 to 17, overshooting the target 10; the next update pulls it back. This rule is gradient descent on the squared error, one example at a time (stochastic gradient descent).

<!-- #endregion lms -->
<!-- #region qlearn -->

$$ \hat Q(s_1, a_{\text{right}}) \leftarrow r + \gamma \max_{a'} \hat Q(s_2, a') = 0 + 0.9 \times \max\{63, 81, 100\} = 0 + 0.9 \times 100 = \mathbf{90} $$

**Why the values fall by 0.9 per step.** With reward 100 only on reaching the goal G and γ = 0.9, a state k moves from G is worth γᵏ × 100:

| Moves to G | Rewards on the way | Discounted value | V* |
|:-:|---|---|---|
| 0 | 100 | 100 | 100 |
| 1 | 0 + 100 | 0.9¹ × 100 | 90 |
| 2 | 0 + 0 + 100 | 0.9² × 100 | 81 |
| 3 | 0 + 0 + 0 + 100 | 0.9³ × 100 | 72.9 |

<!-- #endregion qlearn -->
