</svg>

<p class="fig-cap">More complexity lowers bias and raises variance. The best model sits where the total is lowest, not where either one is zero.</p>

<!-- #endregion biasvar -->
<!-- #region sse -->

The slides’ SSE example, extended to R². Mean of the actual values ȳ = 5.8.

| Actual y | Predicted ŷ | y − ŷ | (y − ŷ)² | y − ȳ | (y − ȳ)² |
|:-:|:-:|--:|--:|--:|--:|
| 5 | 4.8 | 0.2 | 0.04 | −0.8 | 0.64 |
| 7 | 7.5 | −0.5 | 0.25 | 1.2 | 1.44 |
| 3 | 2.9 | 0.1 | 0.01 | −2.8 | 7.84 |
| 6 | 5.7 | 0.3 | 0.09 | 0.2 | 0.04 |
| 8 | 8.2 | −0.2 | 0.04 | 2.2 | 4.84 |
|  |  |  | **SSE = 0.43** |  | **TSS = 14.8** |

$$ R^2 = 1 - \frac{\text{SSE}}{\text{TSS}} = 1 - \frac{0.43}{14.8} = \mathbf{0.9709}, \qquad \text{MSE} = \frac{\text{SSE}}{n} = 0.086, \qquad \text{RMSE} = \sqrt{\text{MSE}} = 0.2933 $$

The model explains 97.1% of the variation in y. RMSE (0.29) is in the same units as y, so it is the error to quote.

<!-- #endregion sse -->
<!-- #region cm -->

A screening test on 200 patients, 40 of whom have the disease. The test flags 50 people, 30 of them really ill.

|  | Predicted ill | Predicted healthy | Total |
|---|:-:|:-:|:-:|
| **Actually ill** | TP = 30 | FN = 10 | 40 |
| **Actually healthy** | FP = 20 | TN = 140 | 160 |
| **Total** | 50 | 150 | 200 |

| Metric | Formula | Value | Reads as |
|---|---|---|---|
| Accuracy | (TP + TN)/total | 170/200 = **0.85** | share of all calls that were right |
| Precision | TP/(TP + FP) | 30/50 = **0.6** | when it says ill, how often it is right |
| Recall (sensitivity, TPR) | TP/(TP + FN) | 30/40 = **0.75** | of the ill, how many it catches |
| F1 | 2PR/(P + R) = 2TP/(2TP + FP + FN) | 60/90 = **0.667** | balance of precision and recall |
| Specificity (TNR) | TN/(TN + FP) | 140/160 = **0.875** | of the healthy, how many it clears |
| FPR | FP/(FP + TN) = 1 − specificity | 20/160 = **0.125** | false alarms among the healthy |

**The accuracy paradox:** a "test" that calls everyone healthy scores 160/200 = **0.8** accuracy, close to this one’s 0.85, while catching **no** ill patient (recall 0). With imbalanced classes, never report accuracy alone.

<!-- #endregion cm -->
<!-- #region roc -->

Eight emails with the model’s spam probability; 4 are spam (1), 4 are not (0): 0.95 (1), 0.85 (1), 0.78 (0), 0.66 (1), 0.6 (0), 0.55 (1), 0.43 (0), 0.3 (0). Use each score as a threshold (spam if p ≥ t):

| Threshold t | TP | FP | FN | TN | TPR | FPR |
|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|--:|--:|
| 0.95 | 1 | 0 | 3 | 4 | 1/4 = 0.25 | 0/4 = 0 |
| 0.85 | 2 | 0 | 2 | 4 | 2/4 = 0.5 | 0/4 = 0 |
| 0.78 | 2 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 2/4 = 0.5 | 1/4 = 0.25 |
| 0.66 | 3 | 1 | 1 | 3 | 3/4 = 0.75 | 1/4 = 0.25 |
| 0.6 | 3 | 2 | 1 | 2 | 3/4 = 0.75 | 2/4 = 0.5 |
| 0.55 | 4 | 2 | 0 | 2 | 4/4 = 1 | 2/4 = 0.5 |
| 0.43 | 4 | 3 | 0 | 1 | 4/4 = 1 | 3/4 = 0.75 |
| 0.3 | 4 | 4 | 0 | 0 | 4/4 = 1 | 4/4 = 1 |

